# --- config tooltips ---
# Generated by tools/lang/build_langs.py config from the @Config comments; rerun it, don't edit.
config.villagerbackport.restock.tooltip=How villagers replenish their trades.
villagerbackport.general.restock.enabled.tooltip=Master switch for timed restocking. When false the mod does nothing and villagers use vanilla 1.12.2 behavior, where a restock only happens after a lucky roll on a successful trade and a villager whose trades all run out can lock permanently.
villagerbackport.general.restock.maxrestocksperday.tooltip=Restocks a villager may perform per day. 1.14's value is 2. This is the main throttle on trade farming: raising it lets a player drain a villager more times per day, and 0 disables restocking as surely as the master switch does.
villagerbackport.general.restock.cooldownticks.tooltip=Ticks a villager must wait between restocks. 1.14's value is 2400 (two minutes). Lowering this makes villagers replenish faster within their daily allowance.
villagerbackport.general.restock.dailyresetticks.tooltip=Ticks of elapsed game time after which a villager's daily restock allowance is refunded, independent of the day/night cycle. 1.14's value is 12000 (half a day). This is the fallback that keeps villagers working on worlds where the daylight cycle is frozen or the time has been moved by a command.
villagerbackport.general.restock.workhoursonly.tooltip=Only let villagers restock during working hours.\n\nThis is what makes 'twice a day' mean twice a day. 1.14 restocks from inside the task that has a villager work at its job site, and that task only runs during the WORK part of the villager schedule - so the daily allowance resets far more often than a villager is ever in a position to spend it. Without the window, a villager stood at its workstation can spend both restocks every time the allowance comes back, right through the night, which is several times a day rather than twice.
villagerbackport.general.restock.playworksound.tooltip=Play a sound each time a villager uses its workstation, about every 15 seconds while it is at work, whether or not it restocks.\n\n1.14 gives each profession its own work sound and plays it as the villager uses its job site. The sound is taken from the workstation rather than the profession here, which comes to the same thing - a librarian is the villager at the lectern - and it means a workstation this mod did not define still makes a noise of the right material.
villagerbackport.general.restock.workstarttime.tooltip=When the working day starts and ends, as time of day in ticks.\n\n1.14's villager schedule turns WORK on at 2000 and off again at 9000, where the villagers gather instead. Dawn is 0 and dusk is 12000. A start later than the end makes a window that runs through midnight, for a pack that has moved the day around.
config.villagerbackport.pricing.tooltip=Supply-and-demand pricing and villager experience.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.demandpricing.tooltip=Adjust prices by demand, the way 1.14 does. A trade used heavily between restocks gets more expensive; one left alone drifts back toward its base price. Demand never discounts below the base price - in 1.14 discounts come from gossip and Hero of the Village, which are not implemented yet. Turning this off leaves every trade at the price its author set.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.pricemultiplier.tooltip=How sharply demand moves prices. 1.14 sets this per-trade, mostly 0.05. The surcharge is floor(basePrice * demand * this), so at 0.05 a trade sold out repeatedly climbs roughly 5%% of its base price per point of accumulated demand. Raising it makes popular trades expensive much faster.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.premiumpricemultiplier.tooltip=How sharply demand and reputation move the price of premium trades. 1.14's value is 0.2.\n\n1.14 stores a multiplier per trade and uses exactly two: 0.05 for ordinary trades and 0.2 for the premium ones. 1.12.2 recipes have no such field, so a trade counts as premium here when what it sells is one of 1.14's premium items: enchanted books, explorer maps, enchanted gear other than the iron sword and bow, and plain iron and chainmail armor, shields, the iron axe, stone tools, the diamond hoe, saddles and leather armor.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.gossipenabled.tooltip=Let villagers form opinions of players, and price accordingly.\n\n1.14's gossip: trading with a villager, hurting one, killing one in front of others and curing one of zombiehood all leave a mark, and a villager gives a discount in proportion to what it thinks of you. Opinions wear off daily and spread between villagers who are stood near each other.\n\nIt runs both ways, as in 1.14: a villager that watched you kill its neighbors will charge you more, and one you cured of zombiehood will charge you less. Only the demand part of the price is floored at zero; this part is signed.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.gossipspreads.tooltip=Let villagers repeat their opinions to each other.\n\n1.14 does this when they gather at the meeting point. An opinion loses strength each time it is retold and a villager keeps only the strongest version it has heard - so a killing travels but arrives weaker, and trading barely travels at all.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.gossiprange.tooltip=How close two villagers must stand to swap gossip, in blocks. 1.14's value is 2.24, the square root of 5: they have to walk right up to each other.\n\nEach villager talks at most once a minute, and hears ten weighted picks from what its partner knows rather than all of it, so news travels round a village over a day or so rather than in minutes.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.maxdemand.tooltip=Ceiling on how far demand can accumulate in either direction. 0 means no ceiling, which is 1.14's behavior and the default.\n\nWith no ceiling a trade that is sold out every day keeps getting dearer until it reaches a full stack, and one left alone for weeks builds up a long way the other way, so it takes as long to start rising again. Set a number to bound both: at 128, a 1-emerald trade at the ordinary rate tops out at 7.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.xpmultiplier.tooltip=Scales the experience villagers earn from trading. 1.0 uses 1.14's own values.\n\n1.14 sets experience per trade, based on the tier the trade unlocked at and which way it goes. Selling goods to a villager pays 2/10/20/30/30 across tiers one to five; buying goods from one pays 1/5/10/15/30. Those are the values used here.\n\nScaling with the tier is what keeps progress steady, because each level costs more than the last - the thresholds are 10, 70, 150 and 250. Raise this to level villagers faster, lower it to make mastery a longer project.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.levelgatedtrades.tooltip=Unlock new trades when a villager levels up, as 1.14 does, instead of when it restocks.\n\n1.12.2 appends a whole new tier of trades roughly every time a villager is traded with - a first trade or one bulk trade is enough. That runs on trade volume alone, so with an experience level also on screen the two disagree: the villager stocks master-tier goods while still reading as Novice.\n\nLeaving this on makes the level mean what it says. Turning it off restores 1.12.2's behavior, where the level is decorative and trades unlock on their own schedule. Restocking is unaffected either way.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.sellingtradestock.tooltip=How many times a trade the player SELLS into can be used before running dry, for trades 1.14 has no figure of its own for. 1.12.2 defaults to 7. Set 0 to leave selling trades as their author made them, including the ones below.\n\nTrades that match one of 1.14's are set to 1.14's own stock for that trade instead: 16 for everyday goods like crops, wool and meat, 12 for valuable ones like iron, gold, diamonds and books.\n\nThis is what makes the experience values above add up. They are 1.14's per-trade amounts, calibrated against 1.14's stock - paying them over less than half the trades means a villager sells out with the next level still out of reach.\n\nTrades this figure applies to are only ever raised to it, so a mod that deliberately made something scarce is not overridden.
villagerbackport.general.pricing.buyingtradestock.tooltip=How many times a trade the player BUYS from can be used before running dry, for trades 1.14 has no figure of its own for. 1.14's usual value is 12; 1.12.2 defaults to 7. Set 0 to leave buying trades untouched, including the ones below.\n\nTrades that match one of 1.14's are set to 1.14's own stock for that trade instead: 3 for every enchanted tool, weapon and piece of armor, 16 for a few everyday goods like bread and wool, 12 for the rest.
config.villagerbackport.trades.tooltip=Which trades villagers offer.
villagerbackport.general.trades.table.tooltip=Which trades new villagers get.\n\nMODERN: 1.14's trade tables. Each level offers two trades picked at random from that level's list, so two farmers can sell different things. 1.14 items 1.12.2 does not have (crossbows, campfires, lanterns, sweet berries, scutes) are only offered when a mod that adds them is installed.\nLEGACY: 1.12.2's own trades, as each career registers them - including any trades another mod has added to a career.\n\nVillagers someone has already traded with keep the trades they have, and keep unlocking from the table they started on, whatever this is set to. Careers another mod adds always use their own trades.
villagerbackport.general.trades.rebuilduntradedvillagers.tooltip=Rebuild the trades of villagers nobody has traded with yet, so they match the table above.\n\nThis is how villages that existed before switching tables get the new trades. A villager anyone has traded with, even once, is never touched.
config.villagerbackport.jobs.tooltip=Workstations villagers go to work at.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.enabled.tooltip=Villagers walk to a workstation to restock, as they do in 1.14. Turning this off removes the behavior entirely - no seeking, no gating.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.requirejobsite.tooltip=Require a villager to be at its workstation before it can restock.\n\nThis is what makes workstations matter rather than being decoration. It fails open on purpose: a career with no workstation block registered is never gated, so a villager can not end up permanently sold out waiting at a block that does not exist. As workstation blocks are added and mapped below, their careers become gated on their own.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.professionsfromworkstations.tooltip=Villagers start with no profession and take one from a workstation, as in 1.14.\n\nA villager with no job has no trades at all. Place a workstation near one and it will claim it and take that job; two villagers can never share a workstation.\n\nLosing the workstation only costs a villager its job if it has never been traded with - 1.14's exact rule is no experience and still at the first level. Trade with one even once and its profession is permanent; it simply cannot restock until it finds another workstation.\n\nVillagers that already have trades or experience when this is first switched on are left alone, so installing the mod on an existing world does not put anyone out of work.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.strictjobsite.tooltip=Also gate villagers whose career HAS a workstation but that have not found one.\n\nOn by default, which is 1.14's rule: a villager only restocks at its own workstation, so a village with no lectern anywhere has librarians that never restock. Turn it off for a world built before this mod was installed, where villages may be missing the blocks; off, villagers restock freely until they find one.\n\nCareers this mod has no workstation for, such as ones added by other mods, are never gated either way.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.searchradius.tooltip=How far a villager will look for an unclaimed workstation, in blocks, in every direction - up and down as far as sideways, as in 1.14.\n\n48 is 1.14's own reach, and this reaches it the same way 1.14 does: the workstations in each chunk are indexed, and a villager queries that index instead of reading the world. Searching the world directly costs its volume, which is what kept this at 12 before and left villagers in a large village permanently jobless - the nearest free workstation was simply further off than they could afford to look.\n\nReach is close to free now: a search at 48 looks at 49 chunks and reads a short list from each. Raising this costs far less than it used to.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.workingdistance.tooltip=How close a villager must be to its workstation to count as working at it.\n\nMeasured from the villager to the middle of the block, so the default of 1.75 means standing against it - straight on or diagonally, on the same level or one step up or down. A block with so much as a one block gap to it scores just over 2 and does not count, which is what keeps a villager from working through a wall.\n\nRaising this past 2 lets villagers work at a distance, walls included.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.extendpathfindingrange.tooltip=Let villagers path as far as they are allowed to look for work.\n\nA navigator will not look for a path further than the entity's follow range, and a villager's is about sixteen blocks - so without this a villager cannot walk to, or even be asked about, anything further off than that. It claims a workstation across the village, gets a path that stops short, and stands at the end of it.\n\nThe extra reach is lent for the one path being worked out and taken straight back. It used to be left in place, which was a bad mistake: follow range is what every path an entity asks for is limited by, so a villager kept at the larger figure also searched a far larger volume every time it wandered, went indoors or fled something. In a village of any size that alone made everything move in jerks.\n\nTurn it off to leave villagers exactly as vanilla pathfinds them, in which case the search radius is best lowered to about 16 to match what they can actually reach.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.modernfarming.tooltip=Farmers farm by 1.14's rules: during work hours only, on the farmland around their composter, harvesting whenever they have room for the crop.\n\nOff, they farm as in 1.12.2: at any hour, any field within 16 blocks of wherever they stand, and only until they have 'enough' food.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.farmradius.tooltip=How far from its composter a farmer farms, in blocks, with modernFarming on. 1.14's farmers reach about 10.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.farmersbakebread.tooltip=Farmers bake bread from wheat when they work at their composter, as in current Minecraft: 3 wheat make 1 bread, up to 3 loaves a visit, and none once they carry more than 36. Bread is what villagers share to breed.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.farmerscompost.tooltip=Farmers compost the seeds they do not need when they work at their composter, as in current Minecraft (added in 1.16): they keep 10 of each kind, compost up to 20 items a visit, and empty a full composter, leaving the bone meal on top of it.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.ignoremobgriefing.tooltip=Let villagers farm and pick up food and seeds even with the mobGriefing gamerule off.\n\nAs in vanilla, a villager only harvests, replants and picks items up while mobGriefing is on, so a world that turns it off to stop creeper damage has farmers standing idle beside ripe fields and villagers ignoring bread thrown to them. On, they do both anyway. Everything else mobGriefing covers stays off, villagers trampling farmland included.
villagerbackport.general.jobs.walkspeed.tooltip=How fast a villager walks to work.
config.villagerbackport.workstations.tooltip=What the workstation blocks accept and produce.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.netheritenamematching.tooltip=Also guess upgrades from item names, for netherite mods that only craft netherite gear from ingots and never offered an upgrade: 'diamond_sword' becomes 'netherite_sword', and the usual spellings of each piece are tried, in every mod that adds a netherite ingot. Gives those items an upgrade path their own mod did not have.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.removecraftingupgrades.tooltip=Remove other mods' crafting-grid netherite upgrades (diamond item plus ingot in a crafting table), so the smithing table is the only way, as in 1.16.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.extracompostables.tooltip=Extra things a composter accepts, beyond 1.14's own list.\n\nEach entry is 'what=chance', where chance is how likely it is to raise the fill level. 1.14 bands its own at 0.3, 0.5, 0.65, 0.85 and 1.0, roughly by how much plant matter is in the thing.\n\n'what' is either an ore dictionary name - 'oredict:treeLeaves' - or an item, as 'modid:name' or 'modid:name:meta'. Ore dictionary entries are the useful ones for mod support: they pick up every mod's leaves, saplings and crops at once without naming any of them, so Dynamic Trees, Better Nether and Rustic are covered by the defaults below without this mod knowing they exist.\n\nEntries naming something that is not installed are ignored.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.derivestonecutterrecipes.tooltip=Work out what the stonecutter can cut by reading the crafting recipes already in the game.\n\nAny recipe that turns some number of one stone block into stairs, slabs, walls or another whole block describes a shape a saw could cut, so reading those backwards produces the whole list - vanilla's and every mod's - without naming a single mod. That is what makes Chisel, Quark, Rustic and anything else work here without a compatibility patch.\n\nRecipes are only accepted when they cannot be turned into a profit: inputs must be whole blocks of rock, and whole-block results must divide exactly and never yield more than they took. Turn this off to use only the entries listed below.\n\nThe list is built once, the first time a stonecutter is opened.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.extrastonecutterrecipes.tooltip=Extra cuts, added on top of whatever was worked out above.\n\nEach entry is 'input=output', where both are 'modid:name' or 'modid:name:meta', and the output may end with '*count' to produce more than one - '*2' is what slabs use.\n\nThe defaults are the four chiseled blocks 1.14 cuts straight from their block. They are listed here rather than worked out because 1.12.2 crafts them from slabs, and deriving from a slab recipe would halve the cost of everything made that way.\n\nEntries naming something that is not installed are ignored.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.derivefletchingrecipes.tooltip=Work out what the fletching table can cut the same way, but for wood.\n\n1.14's fletching table does nothing at all - it gives a villager a job and has no use of its own. This makes it the stonecutter's twin: a log becomes planks, planks become stairs and slabs, in one click and without the offcuts a crafting grid would have given you.\n\nReading the recipes backwards works as well on wood as on stone, and covers every mod's wood without naming any of them.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.extrafletchingrecipes.tooltip=Extra cuts for the fletching table, in the same 'input=output' form as above.\n\nThe defaults are the shapes a crafting grid makes out of planks at a loss - a trapdoor costs six planks for two, a fence gate needs sticks - which the derivation will not take because it only keeps cuts that break even.\n\nEntries naming something that is not installed are ignored.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.blockedfletchinginputs.tooltip=Blocks the fletching table will not accept, whatever the rules decided. Each entry is 'modid:name' or 'modid:name:meta'.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.blockedstonecutterinputs.tooltip=Blocks the stonecutter will not accept, whatever the rules above decided.\n\nEach entry is 'modid:name' or 'modid:name:meta'. Use this if a mod's recipes make the saw offer something a pack would rather it did not.
villagerbackport.general.workstations.loomonlybannerpatterns.tooltip=Make the loom the only way to apply a banner pattern.\n\n1.14 removed patterning from the crafting table outright, so this is what exact 1.14 behavior looks like. It is off by default because it takes something away: an existing world's players and any pack recipe built on the crafting route would both stop working the moment it is turned on.\n\nThe loom itself works either way - this only decides whether the old route also still does. Duplicating a banner in a crafting table is untouched, as it is in 1.14.
config.villagerbackport.villages.tooltip=Which villages generate: 1.14's or 1.12.2's.
villagerbackport.general.villages.modpieces.tooltip=Put other mods' village buildings in 1.14 villages.\n\nMods add buildings to 1.12.2 villages through Forge's village hook: RLCraft's village houses (Recurrent Complex), Waystones' village waystone, and others. 1.14 villages are built from 1.14's own buildings, so without this they have none of them. With it, those buildings are placed beside the village's streets, about as many as they would make up in a 1.12.2 village of the same size.\n\nThe log lists the pieces on offer, by the names modPieceExclusions takes, the first time a 1.14 village is made.
villagerbackport.general.villages.maxmodpieces.tooltip=Most other-mod buildings added to one 1.14 village. The waystone does not count.
villagerbackport.general.villages.waystonenearbell.tooltip=Place Waystones' village waystone first, next to the town center where the bell is, whenever Waystones rolls one for the village (its own 'Generate in Villages' chance). Off, it is just one more piece placed anywhere along the streets.
villagerbackport.general.villages.modpieceexclusions.tooltip=Other mods' village buildings to leave out of 1.14 villages.\n\nEach entry is a mod id ('reccomplex' leaves out all of Recurrent Complex's), a mod id and piece class ('waystones:ComponentVillageWaystone'), or for Recurrent Complex a structure ('reccomplex:P_VillageInn'). The log lists what is on offer.
villagerbackport.general.villages.legacyhouseworkstations.tooltip=In LEGACY villages, put workstations into the houses as they generate: a lectern in the library, a smoker at the butcher's, a blast furnace and smithing table at the blacksmith's, a brewing stand in the church, a composter beside each field, a bell at the well, and one at random in each small house.\n\n1.12.2's village houses have no workstations at all, so without this a 1.12.2 village has almost nowhere for a villager to work.
villagerbackport.general.villages.workstationweights.tooltip=How often each workstation is chosen relative to the others, as 'modid:block=weight'.\n\nAnything not listed weighs 1. A weight of 2 means picked about twice as often, which is a noticeable lean rather than a takeover - with a dozen workstations in the pool a weight of 2 is roughly one in seven rather than one in thirteen.\n\nThe lectern is weighted up because a librarian is the trade most players actually want. Raise a weight to make that job commoner in new villages, or add an entry at 1 to bring one back to level.
villagerbackport.general.villages.excludedworkstations.tooltip=Workstations that may not be built into a village, as 'modid:block'.\n\nThe pool is otherwise every block the workstation mapping above names, so the blocks a village is built with and the blocks a villager will take a job at are always the same set. Use this where a pack would rather a particular job were not handed out for free.
config.villagerbackport.homes.tooltip=Beds, sleeping, breeding and iron golems.
villagerbackport.general.homes.gatheratbell.tooltip=Grown villagers gather at the village bell in the afternoon, as in 1.14.\n\nThey head for the nearest bell within 48 blocks, drift around near it and walk up to each other to mingle, which is also when gossip spreads fastest. A villager with no bell in reach carries on wandering.
villagerbackport.general.homes.gatherstarttime.tooltip=When the gathering starts, in ticks of the day. 1.14's value is 9000 (15:00).
villagerbackport.general.homes.gatherendtime.tooltip=When the gathering ends, in ticks of the day. 1.14's value is 11000 (17:00).
villagerbackport.general.homes.babiesplay.tooltip=Baby villagers play, as in 1.14: from 09:00 to 12:00 and 16:00 to 18:00 they chase each other around the village and bounce on beds.\n\nReplaces 1.12.2's own baby play, which had them follow each other at any hour.
villagerbackport.general.homes.enabled.tooltip=Master switch for beds. When false nothing here happens and villagers behave as 1.12.2 built them: doors decide the village, and nobody sleeps.
villagerbackport.general.homes.sleepatnight.tooltip=Villagers claim a bed and sleep in it at night, as they do from 1.14 onward.\n\n1.12.2 has no sleeping mob of any kind, so this is built rather than enabled: the villager walks to its bed, lies in it until morning, and is drawn lying down.\n\nIt is not decoration. From 1.14 a villager only counts towards an iron golem if it has slept within the last day, so switching this off switches golem spawning off with it - see golemSpawning.
villagerbackport.general.homes.bedsforbreeding.tooltip=Beds rather than doors decide whether a village can grow.\n\n1.12.2 counts doors: villagers breed while the population is under 35%% of the door count, which is why a wall of doors breeds a village. 1.14 counts beds instead, and a baby needs a free one to be born into.\n\nThis gates births on a free bed. It does not remove 1.12.2's own door rule, which still applies first - beds are an additional requirement rather than a replacement, because the door rule lives inside a vanilla AI task that cannot be edited without a coremod.
villagerbackport.general.homes.golemspawning.tooltip=Villagers spawn iron golems when frightened, on 1.14's rules.\n\nA panicking villager tries every 100 ticks and needs two others nearby that are also eligible. Eligible means: grown, slept within the last 24000 ticks, and no golem seen in the last 600. A job is not needed, so nitwits and unemployed villagers count.\n\nThat is why this needs the rest of the mod running. A village whose villagers cannot reach a bed will never produce a golem, as in the game.
villagerbackport.general.homes.searchradius.tooltip=How far a villager will look for a bed, in blocks.
villagerbackport.general.homes.sleepstarttime.tooltip=When villagers go to bed and when they get up, as time of day in ticks.\n\n1.14's schedule turns REST on at 12000 - dusk - and leaves it at daybreak. A start later than the end makes a window running through midnight, which this one does.
villagerbackport.general.homes.debugmessages.tooltip=Report village life in chat: beds claimed, villagers sleeping, gossip exchanged, and every attempt at an iron golem including the ones that come to nothing.\n\nThese are the parts of the mod that cannot be checked by looking. A golem depends on who has slept, who has worked and how many of them are standing together, none of which is visible - so when no golem appears there is otherwise no way to tell which condition was the one that failed. The near misses are the useful half.\n\nOff by default. Only players within 48 blocks are told.
config.villagerbackport.raids.tooltip=Raids, raiders and Hero of the Village, as villagers live through them.
villagerbackport.general.raids.bellrevealsraiders.tooltip=Ringing a bell with raiders nearby makes them glow, as in 1.14.\n\nRaiders are the game's: pillagers, vindicators, evokers, illusioners, ravagers and witches, whichever mod adds them, plus anything in extraRaiders. The resonance sounds a quarter of a second after the ring, and the raiders within 48 blocks light up two seconds after that, for three seconds.
villagerbackport.general.raids.hideonbell.tooltip=Villagers within 32 blocks run home when a bell is rung, as in 1.14.\n\nWith no raid on they stay inside for fifteen seconds, or until fifteen seconds after the bell. During a raid they hide until it is over.
villagerbackport.general.raids.raidbehaviour.tooltip=Villagers live through raids as they do in 1.14.\n\nBefore the first wave they gather at the bell and ring it. Once raiders arrive they go home and stay there. After a victory they come out and celebrate.
villagerbackport.general.raids.herodiscount.tooltip=Hero of the Village takes 30%% off every trade, plus 6.25%% a level, as in 1.14.\n\nCounted from the trade's base price and stacked with reputation, inside the same limits. Needs pricing.demandPricing, which is where prices are set.
villagerbackport.general.raids.raidomen.tooltip=Bad Omen turns into 30 seconds of Raid Omen in a village, and the raid starts when that runs out, as in current Java. Off: the raid starts the moment a player with Bad Omen walks in, as in 1.14.
villagerbackport.general.raids.easywaves.tooltip=How many waves a raid has on easy. 1.14 uses 3.
villagerbackport.general.raids.normalwaves.tooltip=How many waves a raid has on normal. 1.14 uses 5.
villagerbackport.general.raids.hardwaves.tooltip=How many waves a raid has on hard. 1.14 uses 7.
villagerbackport.general.raids.pillagers.tooltip=Which mob a raid sends for each of 1.14's five raiders, by registry name. The first one installed is used; a raider with none installed is left out of the waves.\n\nThis mod's own pillager and ravager come first; another mod's are used instead if they are listed ahead of them.
villagerbackport.general.raids.crossbows.tooltip=The crossbow a pillager carries, by registry name: the first one installed. This mod's own is only there when compat.crossbow adds it. With none of them installed a pillager carries a stand-in that only pillagers can use and that never drops.\n\nThe first of these that players can have is also the one outpost chests hold.
villagerbackport.general.raids.patrols.tooltip=Pillager patrols roam the overworld, as in 1.14: from the fifth day, by day, now and then a band of pillagers appears 24 to 48 blocks from a player who is not in a village, led by a captain with the ominous banner. Only with this mod's raids.
villagerbackport.general.raids.outposts.tooltip=Pillager outposts generate, as in 1.14: a watchtower with tents, logs, targets and iron golem cages around it, manned by pillagers that spawn there day and night. On the village grid, one candidate in five, never within ten chunks of a village. New chunks only. Only with this mod's raids.
villagerbackport.general.raids.outpostsinmoddedbiomes.tooltip=Outposts also generate in other mods' biomes that are like 1.14's outpost biomes: plains, desert, savanna, coniferous forest or snowy flats, but not hills, mountains or water. Off: only the five vanilla biomes 1.14 uses.
villagerbackport.general.raids.vindicators.tooltip=See pillagers.
villagerbackport.general.raids.evokers.tooltip=See pillagers.
villagerbackport.general.raids.witches.tooltip=See pillagers.
villagerbackport.general.raids.ravagers.tooltip=See pillagers.
villagerbackport.general.raids.extraraiders.tooltip=More mobs to treat as raiders, by registry name: e.g. 'somemod:marauder'.\n\nRaiders frighten villagers like a vindicator does and glow when a bell rings.
config.villagerbackport.display.tooltip=What the trade screen shows.
villagerbackport.general.display.announceenchantedbooks.tooltip=Announce in chat which enchantment a villager's new book trades came out as.\n\nWritten when a villager takes a job and rolls its first trades, and when a level-up unlocks a tier carrying more books - so rerolling a librarian by replacing its lectern reports the result without opening the trade screen to look. Only players within 32 blocks are told, and only enchanted books are worth a line.
villagerbackport.general.display.refillkey.tooltip=Key that reloads the selected trade's cost into the trading slots.\n\nNamed as LWJGL names them: SPACE, LALT, TAB, F, and so on. 1.14 uses space, where holding it presses the focused trade button and reloads the trade over and over.\n\nIt is configurable because space is contested. Inventory Tweaks binds its 'move everything' shortcut to space by default, and a shortcut that consumes the click gets there first - which looks exactly like trades being refused while the key is held. If that happens, either rebind this or rebind theirs.\n\nAn unrecognized name falls back to space.
villagerbackport.general.display.autorefillwhiletrading.tooltip=Keep the trading slots loaded automatically while a player is trading.\n\nA deliberate departure from 1.14, where reloading is always something the player asks for. It matters for trades costing more than half a stack - a price of 56 paper against a stack of 64 buys exactly one trade per load - where the alternative is pressing the refill key between every single exchange.\n\nSet false for strict 1.14 behavior: the slots reload when you pick a trade or press the refill key, and at no other time.
villagerbackport.general.display.showvillagerlevel.tooltip=Show a villager's level and experience bar above the trade screen, as 1.14 does. Drawn on top of the vanilla screen rather than replacing it, so mods that patch or decorate the merchant screen keep working. Costs one small packet per trade screen opened. Turning this off also stops that packet being sent.
villagerbackport.general.display.usemoderntradescreen.tooltip=Replace the trade screen with a rebuild of 1.14's: a scrollable list of seven trades down the left, the villager's rank beside its name, and the experience bar.\n\nThis swaps only the screen. Vanilla's merchant container is reused untouched, so mods that patch or hook it keep working, and trade selection still goes over vanilla's own channel - nothing changes server-side.\n\nSet to false to keep the vanilla 1.12.2 screen, with the level shown as a small overlay above it instead.
villagerbackport.general.display.showlevelbadge.tooltip=Show a villager's rank as a badge on its chest, the way 1.14 does - stone, iron, gold, emerald or diamond as it levels from Novice to Master.\n\nDrawn as an extra layer on whatever villager renderer is in use rather than by replacing it, so mods that reshape or decorate villagers keep working.\n\nTurning this off only hides the badge. The villager's level still reaches each player, since its career and biome outfit travel with it.
villagerbackport.general.display.modoutfits.tooltip=Which 1.14 outfit other mods' villagers wear, one per line, as 'profession=outfit' or 'profession/career=outfit'. Professions by registry name, careers by name.\n\nBy default another mod's villager keeps its own texture, with the rank badge drawn on, since there is no telling which part of a whole 1.12.2 skin is the clothes. A line here dresses it as a vanilla villager instead: biome clothing, the outfit, its hat and the badge.\n\nOutfits: armorer, butcher, cartographer, cleric, farmer, fisherman, fletcher, leatherworker, librarian, mason, nitwit, shepherd, toolsmith, weaponsmith, or none for the biome clothing alone.\n\nExamples:\nsomemod:chef=butcher\nminecraft:farmer/beekeeper=farmer   (a career a mod added to a vanilla profession)\n\nA career a mod adds to a vanilla profession wears that profession's outfit unless it is listed here: the farmer's, librarian's, cleric's, toolsmith's or butcher's.
villagerbackport.general.display.showtradeitems.tooltip=Villagers hold up what they would give you for the item in your hand, as in the game. Hold wheat near a farmer that buys wheat and it holds up an emerald; with several trades for the same item it cycles through them. Only when you are within about four blocks, and not while it sleeps or trades.
villagerbackport.general.display.usemodernvillagerskins.tooltip=Give villagers 1.14's appearance - the redrawn body, a biome outfit, and a profession outfit, stacked the way 1.14 stacks them.\n\nPainted over the existing texture rather than replacing the villager renderer, so mods that reshape or decorate villagers keep working.\n\nVillagers whose career has no 1.14 counterpart are left completely alone, so ones added by other mods keep their own look.\n\n1.12.2 has no villager biome type, so the outfit is read from where the villager currently is - meaning one carried far from home will change clothes.
config.villagerbackport.compat.tooltip=Getting along with other mods that add the same blocks.
villagerbackport.general.compat.convertedstayordinary.tooltip=Keep villagers ordinary through zombification and curing.\n\nA zombie villager that was one of your villagers is never made an Infernal Mobs elite, a Scaling Health blight or a Champion, and gets no Scaling Health bonus health. The villager it is cured back into is never turned into a Sussyberian or Mentalberian (Dregora's EagleMixins), which refuse to trade.\n\nWith this on, a Sussyberian or Mentalberian that was once cured is turned back into the librarian it was when its chunk next loads. Ones that generated as berians are left alone. Does nothing for mods that are not installed.
config.villagerbackport.sounds.tooltip=1.14 sounds for vanilla blocks.
villagerbackport.general.sounds.cropsounds.tooltip=Give crops 1.14's sounds: a rustle when seeds are planted, by players and by farmer villagers, and a crunch when a crop is broken.\n\nWheat, carrots, potatoes and beetroot, pumpkin and melon stems, and nether wart, plus any other mod's crop that still uses the plain plant sounds. Takes effect on restart.
config.villagerbackport.trader.tooltip=The wandering trader and its llamas.
villagerbackport.general.trader.enabled.tooltip=Whether this mod adds the wandering trader and trader llamas.
villagerbackport.general.trader.naturalspawning.tooltip=Whether a wandering trader turns up on its own, near a random player in the overworld. Off leaves spawn eggs and /wanderingtrader spawn as the only way to get one. The doTraderSpawning game rule (added to every world, as current Java has it) does the same per world, and doMobSpawning off stops it too.
villagerbackport.general.trader.spawndelay.tooltip=Ticks between spawn attempts. 1.14's value is 24000, one in-game day. The countdown is kept per world and carries on across restarts.
villagerbackport.general.trader.minspawnchance.tooltip=The chance, in percent, of the first attempt after a trader has spawned. Each attempt that fails raises it by spawnChanceStep, up to maxSpawnChance. 1.14: 25, then 50, then 75. Every attempt then has a further one in ten roll before a trader really appears, so the overall chances are 2.5%%, 5%% and 7.5%% - as in 1.14.
villagerbackport.general.trader.spawnchancestep.tooltip=How much each failed attempt raises the chance by, in percent. 1.14's value is 25.
villagerbackport.general.trader.maxspawnchance.tooltip=The most the chance can climb to, in percent. 1.14's value is 75.
villagerbackport.general.trader.spawnradius.tooltip=How far from the chosen player, or from the bell it prefers, a trader may appear, in blocks. Also how far from the player a bell is looked for. 1.14's value is 48.
villagerbackport.general.trader.despawnticks.tooltip=Ticks a naturally spawned trader stays before it leaves. 1.14's value is 48000 (40 minutes). The clock only runs while the trader is loaded and nobody is trading with it. Traders from spawn eggs and commands never leave. 0 means natural ones never leave either.
villagerbackport.general.trader.llamas.tooltip=Trader llamas leashed to each naturally spawned trader. 1.14's value is 2.
villagerbackport.general.trader.oneatatime.tooltip=Whether a new trader waits until the last one has gone. 1.14 only remembers the last trader; with this on, another is not sent while that one is still loaded, or while it could still be somewhere in unloaded chunks (less than despawnTicks since it arrived).
config.villagerbackport.cats.tooltip=Cats, and ocelots as 1.14 changed them.
villagerbackport.general.cats.enabled.tooltip=Whether this mod adds cats, and 1.14's ocelots.
villagerbackport.general.cats.spawninvillages.tooltip=Stray cats turn up in villages, as in 1.14: about once a minute, near a random player, where a village has more than four occupied beds within 48 blocks and fewer than five cats.
villagerbackport.general.cats.spawnatwitchhuts.tooltip=A black cat turns up at a witch hut that has none, as in 1.14.
villagerbackport.general.cats.modernocelots.tooltip=Ocelots behave as in 1.14: they can no longer be tamed, and feeding one raw cod or salmon earns its trust instead, so it stops running from players. Off leaves 1.12.2's ocelots alone.
villagerbackport.general.cats.converttamedocelots.tooltip=Tamed ocelots from before this mod became cats when they load, keeping their owner, name and coat (tuxedo, red and Siamese). This is how 1.14 itself upgraded old worlds.
villagerbackport.general.cats.creepersfearcats.tooltip=Creepers run from cats, as they do from ocelots.
villagerbackport.general.cats.phantomsfearcats.tooltip=Phantoms break off an attack when a cat is within 16 blocks, and the cat hisses. Works with any mod's phantom (anything registered as 'phantom'); 1.12.2 itself has none.
config.villagerbackport.foxes.tooltip=Foxes and sweet berries.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.generatebushes.tooltip=Whether sweet berry bushes generate in taigas, as in 1.14.\n\nTaiga-like biomes are found by the Forge biome dictionary (CONIFEROUS), so modded taigas get them too. Nothing generates in a biome listed in bushBiomeBlacklist.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.taigapatchesperchunk.tooltip=Bush patches tried per chunk in an ordinary (not snowy, not giant) taiga.\n\n1.14 tries one patch per chunk here, at a random height up to twice the surface, so only some of them land on the ground. That is copied as it is.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.rarepatchchance.tooltip=One chunk in this many gets a bush patch in a snowy or giant tree taiga.\n\n1.14's snowy taigas use one in twelve. 1.14.4 has no bushes in giant tree taigas; current Java does, rarely, so they share the snowy taiga's rate. 0 turns both off.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.bushbiomeblacklist.tooltip=Biomes (registry names, like minecraft:taiga) that never get sweet berry bushes.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.spawnweight.tooltip=Fox spawn weight in taiga-like biomes. 1.14 uses 8, the same as wolves in a taiga. 0 stops foxes spawning naturally; spawn eggs and breeding still work.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.mingroup.tooltip=The smallest group of foxes that spawns together. 1.14 uses 2.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.maxgroup.tooltip=The largest group of foxes that spawns together. 1.14 uses 4.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.extrafoxbiomes.tooltip=Biomes (registry names) foxes spawn in, on top of every CONIFEROUS biome. A snowy biome gets snow foxes, any other red ones.
villagerbackport.general.foxes.foxbiomeblacklist.tooltip=Biomes (registry names) foxes never spawn in, even when they are CONIFEROUS.
config.villagerbackport.bamboo.tooltip=Bamboo, scaffolding, the bamboo jungle and pandas.
villagerbackport.general.bamboo.bamboojungle.tooltip=Generate the bamboo jungle and bamboo jungle hills biomes.\n\nAs in 1.14 they are not biomes of their own climate: some jungles are bamboo jungles instead, and some jungle hills bamboo jungle hills. Works with any world type that places vanilla's jungle - the default, large biomes, amplified, and most biome mods. World types that do not use vanilla's biome layers (Open Terrain Generator presets, for one) never contain them.\n\nTurning this off also leaves the two biomes unregistered.
villagerbackport.general.bamboo.bamboojunglerarity.tooltip=One jungle area in this many becomes a bamboo jungle.\n\n1.14 turns one jungle in ten into a bamboo jungle. Lower is more common.
villagerbackport.general.bamboo.pandajungleweight.tooltip=Spawn weight of pandas in ordinary jungles and jungle hills (1.14: 1).\n\nPandas mostly live in bamboo jungles; this is the rare one met elsewhere. 0 keeps them out of ordinary jungles.
villagerbackport.general.bamboo.pandabamboojungleweight.tooltip=Spawn weight of pandas in bamboo jungles (1.14: 80).
config.villagerbackport.campfire.tooltip=The campfire.
villagerbackport.general.campfire.cooktime.tooltip=How long one item takes to cook, in ticks. 1.14's is 600 (30 seconds), three times a furnace.
villagerbackport.general.campfire.extrafoods.tooltip=What the campfire cooks. By default: every furnace recipe whose input and output are both food, which is 1.14's own list (raw meat, raw fish, potatoes) plus any mod's raw foods.\n\nAdd an item the default leaves out as 'modid:item' (cooked as the furnace would) or 'modid:item=modid:result' (cooked into that). Add ':meta' after either to pick one variant, e.g. 'minecraft:fish:1=minecraft:cooked_fish:1'.
villagerbackport.general.campfire.excludedfoods.tooltip=Items the campfire refuses, even though the rule above would cook them, as 'modid:item' or 'modid:item:meta'.
config.villagerbackport.flora.tooltip=1.14's flowers, dyes, suspicious stew and leather horse armor.
villagerbackport.general.flora.generateflowers.tooltip=Generate cornflowers in plains, sunflower plains and flower forests, and lilies of the valley in forests, birch forests, dark forests and flower forests, as 1.14 does. Modded biomes join in by their biome dictionary types: plains that are not snowy or savanna get cornflowers, forests that are not coniferous, snowy or jungle get lilies.\n\nAlso decides whether bone meal on grass can grow them (cornflowers in plains, sunflower plains and flower forests, lilies only in flower forests).
villagerbackport.general.flora.brownmooshrooms.tooltip=Brown mooshrooms. Lightning turns a red mooshroom brown and a brown one red. Feed a brown one a small flower, then milk it with a bowl, for that flower's suspicious stew. Sheared, it drops brown mushrooms. Red mooshrooms are unchanged.
villagerbackport.general.flora.dyeablesigns.tooltip=Right-clicking a sign with any dye recolors its text, as in 1.14. The color is kept with the sign and drawn on every client that has this mod.
villagerbackport.general.flora.witherkillsplantroses.tooltip=A mob, player or villager the Wither kills leaves a wither rose: planted where it died if the block is free and mobGriefing allows it, dropped as an item otherwise.
config.villagerbackport.building.tooltip=1.14's stone slabs, stairs and walls, smooth stone and wood signs.
villagerbackport.general.building.enabled.tooltip=Whether this mod adds 1.14's building blocks: the new stone, sandstone, quartz, nether brick and end stone brick slabs, stairs and walls, smooth stone, smooth sandstone, smooth red sandstone, smooth quartz, and signs in the five other woods.\n\nTurning it off in a world that has them deletes them.
villagerbackport.general.building.vanillarecipechanges.tooltip=Change seven vanilla recipes the way 1.14 did, so the new blocks can be crafted.\n\nsign                any planks -> oak planks only (the other woods make their own)\nstone slab          stone -> smooth stone (stone now makes 1.14's plain stone slab)\nsandstone slab      no longer takes cut sandstone (that makes the cut slab)\nred sandstone slab  no longer takes cut red sandstone (likewise)\nstone brick slab    plain stone bricks only (mossy ones make their own)\nstone brick stairs  likewise\nnether brick fence  4 nether brick blocks and 2 nether bricks (6 blocks is the wall)\n\nWithout this, each pair shares one crafting pattern and only one of the two can ever be crafted. Off leaves vanilla's recipes exactly as they were. Does nothing when building.enabled is off.
config.villagerbackport.villageBlocks.tooltip=1.14's stripped logs, wood trapdoors, buttons and pressure plates, blue ice and sea pickles.
# --- end config tooltips ---
